---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import KeyDates from '../components/KeyDates.astro';
import election from '../data/election.json';
---

<Base
  title="How to Vote — Clacton By-Election 2026"
  description="How to vote in the Clacton by-election on 13 August 2026: deadlines to register and to apply for a postal or proxy vote, photo ID rules, and what happens on polling day."
>
  <h1>How to vote</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Polling day is Thursday 13 August 2026, with {election.pollingStations} polling
    stations open from 7am to 10pm across the constituency. You can vote if you
    live in the Clacton constituency, are registered to vote, are 18 or over on
    polling day, and are a British, Irish or qualifying Commonwealth citizen.
  </p>

  <KeyDates />

  <h2>1. Register to vote — by midnight on 28 July</h2>
  <p>
    Registering takes about five minutes
    at <a href="https://www.gov.uk/register-to-vote" rel="noopener">gov.uk/register-to-vote</a>.
    You need your National Insurance number. If you are already registered
    (for example, you voted in the May 2026 Essex county elections) you do not
    need to register again. The deadline to be on the register for this
    by-election is <strong>midnight on Tuesday 28 July 2026</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Bring photo ID</h2>
  <p>
    You must show photo ID to vote in person. Accepted forms include a
    passport, driving licence (including provisional), blue badge, older
    person's bus pass, PASS card and others on
    the <a href="https://www.electoralcommission.org.uk/voting-and-elections/voter-id" rel="noopener">Electoral Commission's full list</a>.
    Expired ID is accepted if the photo still looks like you. If you have none
    of the accepted documents, apply for a
    free <a href="https://www.gov.uk/apply-for-photo-id-voter-authority-certificate" rel="noopener">Voter
    Authority Certificate</a> by <strong>5pm on Wednesday 5 August 2026</strong>.
    Photo ID is not needed for postal voting.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Choose how to vote</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>In person</strong> — polling stations are open 7am to 10pm on
      Thursday 13 August. Your polling station is printed on the poll card
      posted to you; you do not need the poll card itself to vote, but you do
      need photo ID. If you are in the queue at 10pm you will still be able to
      vote.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>By post</strong> — apply
      at <a href="https://www.gov.uk/apply-postal-vote" rel="noopener">gov.uk/apply-postal-vote</a>.
      New applications must be received by <strong>5pm on Wednesday 29 July 2026</strong>.
      Completed postal votes must reach the returning officer by 10pm on
      polling day; you can also hand them in at a polling station in the
      constituency or at the council offices, with a completed form, and there
      are limits on how many other people's postal votes you may hand in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>By proxy</strong> — someone you trust votes on your behalf.
      Apply at <a href="https://www.gov.uk/apply-proxy-vote" rel="noopener">gov.uk/apply-proxy-vote</a> by
      <strong>5pm on Wednesday 5 August 2026</strong>. Your proxy must bring
      their own photo ID. If an emergency after that deadline — such as a
      medical incident — stops you voting in person, you can apply for an
      emergency proxy up to 5pm on polling day.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Marking a long ballot paper</h2>
  <p>
    With a record {election.candidateCount} candidates standing, the ballot
    paper is unusually long, and candidates are listed alphabetically by
    surname. Mark <strong>one</strong> cross, in the box next to the single
    candidate you are voting for. Marking more than one box makes the paper
    void, and any writing that identifies you can also spoil it. If you make a
    mistake before putting the paper in the box, ask staff for a replacement.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions about your registration?</h2>
  <p>
    Electoral services for the constituency are run
    by <a href={election.councilElectionsUrl} rel="noopener">Tendring District Council</a> —
    they can confirm whether you are registered, where your polling station
    is, and the status of a postal or proxy application. The acting returning
    officer for the by-election is {election.returningOfficer}.
  </p>

  <div class="notice">
    <strong>The count starts once polls close at 10pm on 13 August.</strong>
    {' '}With almost 80,000 electors eligible and {election.candidateCount} candidates
    to count, the declaration is expected in the early hours of Friday 14 August.
  </div>
</Base>
